    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d973">

                <pb facs="1147" n="1037" xml:id="pg1037"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/1553: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-07T21:00:00+08:00">March 7, 1931—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 8—1:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Department’s written instruction number 1467, February 3, 1930.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d973-f1"/> I discussed informally with Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    this morning possibility of negotiation of an agreement for the Claims
                    Commission. Doctor Wang’s first reaction was that Chinese Government would
                    object on the ground that such a commission would interfere with work of the
                    courts but admitted that question appeared novel to him and that he would like
                    to study it.</p>
                <p>I am therefore giving him for study without commitment on either side copy of
                    draft of protocol<anchor n="8" xml:id="d973-f2"/> enclosed with the Department’s
                    instruction.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
